Rune's web host keeps the modifier state from winit's `ModifiersChanged` to itself, and winit's web
backend sends that event after the key's `KeyboardInput`. So the runtime's held modifiers
(`Tx::mods`) are one key behind in the browser: Shift reads as up while held and as down after
it is released. Pointer handlers then miss Shift (15° snapping) and Ctrl (no binding). This
forwards the change to the runtime as the native host does. Drop this file once rune does that
upstream.

--- a/crates/rune/src/platform/web/input.rs
+++ b/crates/rune/src/platform/web/input.rs
@@ -134,7 +134,13 @@
         let mut activate = false;
         let mut immediate = false;
         match event {
-            WindowEvent::ModifiersChanged(mods) => { self.input.mods = mods.state(); return false; }
+            WindowEvent::ModifiersChanged(mods) => {
+                // winit's web backend sends a key's ModifiersChanged after its KeyboardInput, so
+                // the key event carried the old flags. Tell the runtime, as the native host does,
+                // so `Tx::mods` in pointer handlers sees the keys held now.
+                self.input.mods = mods.state();
+                return self.rt.physical_observation(crate::PhysicalInput::Modifiers(modifiers(self.input.mods)));
+            }
             WindowEvent::CursorMoved { position, .. } if self.input.touch.is_none() => {
                 self.rt.pointer_move(position.x as f32 / scale, position.y as f32 / scale);
                 self.cursor.apply(window, self.rt.cursor(), true);
